WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:00.910
<v Speaker 1>Welcome to you.

2
00:00:00.910 --> 00:00:09.470
<v Speaker 1>It is wonderful to have you with us as we open the pages of establishing the Imam, working our way through the profound arguments laid out by the author.

3
00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:12.590
<v Speaker 1>We are at a crucial turning point in the text.

4
00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:20.030
<v Speaker 1>He now finds the imam hidden inside the everyday duties of Islam, so prayer, alms, and war quietly require him.

5
00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:24.070
<v Speaker 2>Take the imam away, and the prayer of the alms of the war do not lose a leader.

6
00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:24.990
<v Speaker 2>They fall void.

7
00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:26.350
<v Speaker 1>I mean, that is the core of it.

8
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:29.710
<v Speaker 1>That is the entire anchor of the argument the author is building for us.

9
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:30.270
<v Speaker 2>It really is.

10
00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:42.950
<v Speaker 1>He is taking the argument for the imam out of those abstract celestial realms of pure theology-like, the kinds of philosophical altitudes that are breathtaking but often feel removed from daily existence.

11
00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:47.150
<v Speaker 1>and he is dropping it squarely into the physical daily acts of the religion.

12
00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:47.950
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, exactly.

13
00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:53.710
<v Speaker 2>Because if we look at the preceding arguments the author made, they established what we might call the ceiling of his claim.

14
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:59.070
<v Speaker 2>He argued methodically that the leader of the time is the absolute summit of the human world.

15
00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:02.590
<v Speaker 2>But the author understands human nature.

16
00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:06.270
<v Speaker 2>He knows that an argument resting solely at that high altitude

17
00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:10.110
<v Speaker 2>can feel utterly remote to the person living their everyday life.

18
00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:16.830
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, it runs the risk of being perceived as, just a beautiful piece of cosmic reasoning that never actually touches the ground you walk on.

19
00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:17.630
<v Speaker 2>Precisely.

20
00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:19.070
<v Speaker 2>So he brings it down to earth.

21
00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:27.830
<v Speaker 2>We were talking about the daily prayers, the handling of charity, the administration of justice in the courtroom, and well, the brutal reality of the battlefield.

22
00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:33.790
<v Speaker 2>The leader is the physical and spiritual hinge on which every single duty turns.

23
00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:42.430
<v Speaker 1>To build that case, to truly show how this authority is wired into our daily reality, the author actually starts at the absolute beginning of human experience.

24
00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:44.510
<v Speaker 1>He begins with a crisis of ignorance.

25
00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:45.470
<v Speaker 2>First principles.

26
00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:47.030
<v Speaker 2>We have to start with first principles.

27
00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:47.550
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

28
00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:49.230
<v Speaker 1>A newborn baby knows nothing.

29
00:01:49.230 --> 00:01:52.350
<v Speaker 1>That is just an observable, irrefutable fact of biology.

30
00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:55.070
<v Speaker 1>Human beings arrive completely empty of knowledge.

31
00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.390
<v Speaker 2>It is a profound vulnerability.

32
00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:03.150
<v Speaker 2>And the author points out that scripture states this starting point without any softening at all.

33
00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:07.630
<v Speaker 2>We are brought forth from the wombs of our mothers, knowing absolutely nothing.

34
00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:09.310
<v Speaker 1>We are entirely destitute.

35
00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:10.270
<v Speaker 2>Utterly destitute.

36
00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:16.670
<v Speaker 2>And only afterward are we handed the instruments of learning, hearing, sight, and hearts.

37
00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:24.110
<v Speaker 2>So to prove that knowledge explicitly requires instruction, that it does not just spontaneously well up from our biology,

38
00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:27.950
<v Speaker 2>I love this part.

39
00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:31.550
<v Speaker 2>He asks us to consider the donkey's bray and the dog's bark.

40
00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:37.310
<v Speaker 1>It is such a striking comparison, focusing purely on what nature produces without any outside interference.

41
00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:41.710
<v Speaker 2>It is almost jarring, but I mean, it does exactly what it needs to do logically.

42
00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:46.750
<v Speaker 2>He says, if knowledge and speech came purely from nature, then human beings would speak perfectly by nature.

43
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:52.990
<v Speaker 2>in the exact same way a donkey brays and a dog barks without anyone ever having to pull them aside and teach it to them.

44
00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.830
<v Speaker 1>Right, because nature never varies.

45
00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:55.870
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

46
00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:02.430
<v Speaker 2>The braying of a donkey sounds identical whether you are standing in India or in the country of the Arabs.

47
00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:06.430
<v Speaker 2>The bark of a dog is exactly the same everywhere on earth.

48
00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:07.470
<v Speaker 1>It's programmed.

49
00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:09.150
<v Speaker 2>It belongs strictly to nature.

50
00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:13.870
<v Speaker 2>But human speech completely defies that uniformity.

51
00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.110
<v Speaker 2>It splinters into many tongues.

52
00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:21.150
<v Speaker 1>A child raised in one part of the world speaks an entirely different language than a child raised in another.

53
00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.350
<v Speaker 1>We do not have a universal natural bark.

54
00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:25.470
<v Speaker 2>No, we do not.

55
00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:31.550
<v Speaker 2>Because we must be taught this capacity for communication splinters based entirely on who is doing the teaching.

56
00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.350
<v Speaker 2>And knowledge behaves in the exact same way.

57
00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:38.910
<v Speaker 2>If it just welled up from nature, everyone on earth would know the exact same things equally.

58
00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:40.190
<v Speaker 1>Manifestly we do not.

59
00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:42.350
<v Speaker 1>We are completely dependent on being instructed.

60
00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:45.630
<v Speaker 2>And that dependency is the foundation stone of his entire proof.

61
00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:52.990
<v Speaker 2>Because if every single person must learn from another person, we are compelled by simple logic to follow that chain of instruction backward.

62
00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:53.950
<v Speaker 1>Link after link.

63
00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.950
<v Speaker 2>Link after link stretching back through time.

64
00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.910
<v Speaker 2>But reason dictates that a chain of human teachers cannot regress forever into infinity.

65
00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:02.350
<v Speaker 2>It must end somewhere.

66
00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:05.790
<v Speaker 1>It cannot simply end in just another human learner.

67
00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:11.390
<v Speaker 2>No, because then you are immediately forced to ask who taught them, and the chain just keeps going.

68
00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:16.030
<v Speaker 2>It must logically end in one who is taught by no human being at all.

69
00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:21.390
<v Speaker 2>It must end in someone who received knowledge through direct divine transmission.

70
00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:23.150
<v Speaker 1>The ultimate end point.

71
00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:26.350
<v Speaker 2>That final teacher who anchors the entire human chain

72
00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:30.910
<v Speaker 2>peace, our blessings of Allah be upon him and his family and the imam.

73
00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:34.670
<v Speaker 1>So our initial ignorance as human beings is completely deliberate then.

74
00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:37.310
<v Speaker 1>Like we are built to need this chain.

75
00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:38.190
<v Speaker 1>Utterly deliberate.

76
00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:40.990
<v Speaker 2>God made human beings ignorant by design.

77
00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:49.630
<v Speaker 2>If we were created already complete, there would be absolutely nothing to teach us, no moral reward to earn, and frankly, no wisdom in creating us to begin with.

78
00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:53.950
<v Speaker 1>Which leads us directly into the author's principle that the earth is never left empty of this guide.

79
00:04:55.190 --> 00:05:05.470
<v Speaker 2>Because if humanity requires a teacher to rescue us from our ignorance and to resolve our inevitable disputes, because human knowledge splinters, then removing that teacher leaves the whole community in chaos.

80
00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:09.150
<v Speaker 1>It creates A theological disaster regarding the justice of God.

81
00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:09.790
<v Speaker 2>It does.

82
00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:15.550
<v Speaker 2>We could stand before him and say, you designed us to need instruction, yet we found no one to guide us.

83
00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.510
<v Speaker 2>That is philosophically unthinkable.

84
00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:20.830
<v Speaker 2>So God's standing proof is always present.

85
00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:28.910
<v Speaker 1>As the commander of the faithful, peace be upon him, firmly established in his teachings, the earth is never empty of one who stands for God with the proof.

86
00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:29.630
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

87
00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:39.390
<v Speaker 2>Whether that figure is visible and widely recognized, or whether he is fearful and hidden from public view due to the dangers of the time, the proof is always physically present.

88
00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:44.430
<v Speaker 1>So we have established this absolute necessity for a defining center of knowledge.

89
00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:48.590
<v Speaker 1>And then the author takes a profound turn to illustrate how the center operates.

90
00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:52.430
<v Speaker 1>He introduces 3 symbols to explain the structure of this leadership.

91
00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:54.950
<v Speaker 1>The circle, the number one, and the dot.

92
00:05:55.150 --> 00:06:00.910
<v Speaker 2>Yes, he turns to geometry and mathematics to make a bold claim about the architecture of reality itself.

93
00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:02.670
<v Speaker 1>Let us look at the geometry first.

94
00:06:02.750 --> 00:06:05.870
<v Speaker 2>Among all the figures, the highest and most encompassing is the circle.

95
00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:12.670
<v Speaker 2>The circle is the DAA, the bounding figure, the circle of religion.

96
00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:16.350
<v Speaker 2>Every other shape can be logically and physically derived from the circle.

97
00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:19.750
<v Speaker 1>If you divide a circle, you can find a perfect square inside it.

98
00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:21.710
<v Speaker 1>Triangles emerge from its points.

99
00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:26.510
<v Speaker 1>Every other shape is contained within it, but the circle itself depends on absolutely none of them.

100
00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:28.910
<v Speaker 2>The traffic of dependency only runs one way.

101
00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:32.510
<v Speaker 1>The circle generates the square, but the square cannot generate the circle.

102
00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:33.150
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

103
00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.710
<v Speaker 2>And the author applies this geometric absolute directly to the

104
00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:38.030
<v Speaker 2>the hierarchy of leadership.

105
00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:40.270
<v Speaker 2>The leader is the circle of religion.

106
00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:46.990
<v Speaker 2>He needs no other rank to justify his existence, but every other rank derives their reality entirely from him.

107
00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:50.110
<v Speaker 1>He runs this same logic through the science of numbers too.

108
00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:53.070
<v Speaker 2>The absolute foundation of all counting is the number one.

109
00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:54.590
<v Speaker 2>Two is just 1 + 1.

110
00:06:54.910 --> 00:07:00.350
<v Speaker 2>Every larger number depends entirely on the repetition of the unit, but the unit needs no other number to exist.

111
00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:02.270
<v Speaker 1>And then finally, the dot.

112
00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:03.710
<v Speaker 2>The finest symbol of all.

113
00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:11.870
<v Speaker 2>Philosophers describe the dot as having no parts, no extension, no width, no depth, yet it is the absolute origin of the line.

114
00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:15.070
<v Speaker 2>The line generates the surface, the surface generates the solid body.

115
00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:20.030
<v Speaker 2>Everything physically extended in the universe is generated from a point that has no extension at all.

116
00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:24.110
<v Speaker 1>That sounds like wordplay, reading the imam into every right after the fact.

117
00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:32.870
<v Speaker 1>It is very elegant poetry, taking geometry into numbers and mapping A theological hierarchy onto them, but it feels heavily contrived.

118
00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:34.230
<v Speaker 1>It feels like he has the conclusion

119
00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:35.550
<v Speaker 1>inclusion he wants to reach.

120
00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:39.950
<v Speaker 1>And he is just scouring mathematics for metaphors to justify it.

121
00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:41.310
<v Speaker 2>Is a fair challenge.

122
00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:45.470
<v Speaker 2>And the brilliant thing is that the author anticipates it entirely.

123
00:07:45.550 --> 00:07:47.070
<v Speaker 1>Oh, he sees it coming.

124
00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:49.230
<v Speaker 2>He knows that geometry can feel abstract.

125
00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:52.350
<v Speaker 2>Metaphors can be dismissed as intellectual tricks.

126
00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:58.430
<v Speaker 2>So he leaves the realm of shapes and numbers and walks us straight out of the academy and into the mosque.

127
00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:04.990
<v Speaker 2>He grounds the argument in the most basic, inescapable, daily physical gesture of Muslim worship.

128
00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:06.750
<v Speaker 1>The direction you face when you pray.

129
00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:07.630
<v Speaker 2>The Qabilah.

130
00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:11.390
<v Speaker 2>He asks us to look at the very language, the physical command itself.

131
00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:15.070
<v Speaker 2>The word Qibla comes from an Arabic root that means face to face.

132
00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:18.190
<v Speaker 1>To turn toward it is to set your face directly in front of something.

133
00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:19.310
<v Speaker 2>Intrinsically, yes.

134
00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:25.950
<v Speaker 2>And the root for being before something, standing in front of it and preceding it is the exact same root that forms the word imam.

135
00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:26.910
<v Speaker 1>I see.

136
00:08:26.990 --> 00:08:32.590
<v Speaker 2>The prayer leader is called that precisely because he stands before the rows and precedes them.

137
00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:38.030
<v Speaker 2>So the divine command to face forward in prayer is not merely geographical.

138
00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:41.110
<v Speaker 1>It is not just about aligning your body with coordinates on a map.

139
00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:44.510
<v Speaker 2>It is a command to orient yourself toward the one who goes before you.

140
00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:47.470
<v Speaker 2>When you turn your face forward in prayer,

141
00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:56.910
<v Speaker 2>You are performing in a physical miniature what your entire life of worship is meant to be, a deliberate turning of the self toward the one appointed to lead it.

142
00:08:56.910 --> 00:09:02.590
<v Speaker 1>I don't buy that yet a shared root doesn't make facing the Qibla an argument about who leads.

143
00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:06.150
<v Speaker 1>Languages share roots all the time through historical accident.

144
00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:13.950
<v Speaker 1>You cannot build a theological mandate for absolute infallible leadership just because two words share a few consonants in classical Arabic.

145
00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:16.830
<v Speaker 2>And again, the author is ready for that exact resistance.

146
00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:19.070
<v Speaker 2>He pushes much further than just etymology.

147
00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:23.630
<v Speaker 2>He moves from the direction of the prayer to the rigorous mechanics of the congregation itself.

148
00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:25.470
<v Speaker 1>Okay, let us look at the congregation.

149
00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:32.990
<v Speaker 2>Think about the well-known prophetic teaching that a prayer performed in congregation is worth 27 times a solitary prayer.

150
00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:37.470
<v Speaker 2>The author asks, where does that enormous multiplier come from?

151
00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:40.270
<v Speaker 1>Why such a massive difference in spiritual value?

152
00:09:40.270 --> 00:09:40.910
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

153
00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:44.590
<v Speaker 2>He argues it is not simply the physical presence of a crowd that earns it.

154
00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:49.310
<v Speaker 2>God is not rewarding you just for standing shoulder to shoulder in a crowded room.

155
00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:51.710
<v Speaker 1>It stems entirely from the presence of the leader.

156
00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:59.630
<v Speaker 2>It stems from the fact that the prayers performed behind him, facing him, being led by him in intention as well as in physical action.

157
00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:06.350
<v Speaker 1>Let us actually unpack that requirement of intention because it is central to how the author views allegiance.

158
00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:10.030
<v Speaker 1>It is not just about moving your body when the leader moves his body.

159
00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:10.510
<v Speaker 2>Not at all.

160
00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:13.710
<v Speaker 2>It is a rigid, unforgiving spiritual discipline.

161
00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:21.790
<v Speaker 2>If A follower in the congregation copies the leader's movements exactly, bowing the moment he bows, prostrating the moment he prostrates,

162
00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:25.310
<v Speaker 2>but their inner intention is not completely joined to his leadership.

163
00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:27.230
<v Speaker 2>Their prayer is entirely void.

164
00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:27.870
<v Speaker 1>Wow.

165
00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:32.350
<v Speaker 1>I mean, if you consciously oppose the leader in a single bow, your prayer is broken.

166
00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:33.550
<v Speaker 2>You must start over.

167
00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:38.510
<v Speaker 2>The outward match of the physical limbs is completely worthless without the inward match of intention.

168
00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:41.950
<v Speaker 2>And this is a profoundly reciprocal relationship.

169
00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:46.830
<v Speaker 2>If the follower gives their intention completely, the leader becomes their guarantor.

170
00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:49.790
<v Speaker 1>The leader compensates for the follower's hidden negligence.

171
00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:51.710
<v Speaker 2>Yes, covering their internal lapses.

172
00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:56.470
<v Speaker 2>He carries the spiritual weight of what the follower inadvertently drops.

173
00:10:56.470 --> 00:10:58.750
<v Speaker 1>So the lesser case proves the greater case.

174
00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:00.350
<v Speaker 2>That is the logical leap.

175
00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:10.590
<v Speaker 2>If obedience to a mere local, fallible prayer leader is this weighty, if opposing him in a single physical gesture annuls your conversation with God,

176
00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:14.830
<v Speaker 2>then what must logically be the case with the supreme divine leader?

177
00:11:14.990 --> 00:11:20.990
<v Speaker 1>The author is saying the structure of the daily prayer is actually a working daily model of absolute allegiance.

178
00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:22.110
<v Speaker 1>It is training us.

179
00:11:22.190 --> 00:11:25.230
<v Speaker 2>A working model that scales up to every major public right.

180
00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:28.510
<v Speaker 2>He extends this same structural necessity of the Friday prayers.

181
00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:32.070
<v Speaker 2>He notes that the Friday congregation is entirely invalid without the sermon.

182
00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:32.990
<v Speaker 1>The KHU Bob.

183
00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:33.550
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

184
00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:35.870
<v Speaker 2>The prayer itself cannot occur without it.

185
00:11:36.590 --> 00:11:42.750
<v Speaker 2>And that sermon cannot be validly delivered except by the leader or his explicitly appointed representative.

186
00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:48.190
<v Speaker 1>Strip him away, and the community's central weekly gathering just dissolves into a meaningless gathering.

187
00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:54.190
<v Speaker 2>And he scales it up even further, taking us out of the local mosque and mapping this logic onto the ultimate journey.

188
00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:55.670
<v Speaker 2>pilgrimage.

189
00:11:56.230 --> 00:12:01.990
<v Speaker 1>The physical reality of where the Kaaba is located becomes a vital part of the proof for the nature of leadership.

190
00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:04.870
<v Speaker 2>The geographical setting is deeply intentional.

191
00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:13.430
<v Speaker 2>God placed his house in a barren desert, a valley entirely without cultivation, devoid of any worldly benefit or comfort.

192
00:12:13.750 --> 00:12:17.390
<v Speaker 1>Historically, a place reachable only through intense physical hardship.

193
00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:20.870
<v Speaker 2>Weariness, risk of harm from enemies and elements along the road.

194
00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:30.790
<v Speaker 1>When you think about it from a purely human perspective, why not put the absolute center of pilgrimage somewhere accessible, a place of peace and prosperity where worship could be conducted in comfort?

195
00:12:30.950 --> 00:12:35.030
<v Speaker 2>So that the pilgrim would understand that what the house truly symbolizes

196
00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:37.830
<v Speaker 2>can only be reached in the exact same way.

197
00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:44.310
<v Speaker 2>The leader is reached only through immense effort, endurance, and the absolute willingness to suffer for the journey.

198
00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:49.830
<v Speaker 1>Placing the Kaaba in a barren valley filters the motive of every single person who approaches it.

199
00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:52.150
<v Speaker 2>Guarantees the sincerity of the seeker.

200
00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:56.550
<v Speaker 2>The turning toward the leader must arise from pure faith alone.

